WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:04.380
Could a post-slap Will Smith win another Oscar?

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.879
Is "Emancipation" based on a true story?

00:00:06.879 --> 00:00:10.219
Here's all you need to know before seeing
Smith's next big film.

00:00:10.734 --> 00:00:15.900
In just a few months, Will Smith's historical
drama "Emancipation" will be hitting theaters

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:22.590
and dropping on Apple TV+, and there are already
people with extremely high hopes for the emotionally-charged

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.130
action epic.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:29.080
Based on a true story, "Emancipation" is intended
to shine a light on the United States' most

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:34.507
turbulent time in history — the American
Civil War and the height of slavery in the South.

00:00:34.507 --> 00:00:39.230
Early reactions to the film have so far been
very strong, with most people who've seen

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:44.309
it offering up immense praise for the subject
matter and themes on social media.

00:00:44.309 --> 00:00:49.020
According to Deadline, a private screening
of "Emancipation" was held in early October

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.699
by Apple Original Films and the NAACP.

00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:57.250
Members of the Congressional Black Caucus,
Historically Black College and Universities,

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:03.430
the Divine 9, National Council of Negro Women,
the National Coalition on Black Civic Participation,

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.269
and other prominent groups were invited.

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:11.020
On October 24, Smith also held a private screening
of "Emancipation" for some of his celebrity

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:17.729
friends, including Rihanna, Dave Chapelle,
and A$AP Rocky. Smith later took to his Instagram

00:01:17.729 --> 00:01:20.290
page to commemorate the event, saying:

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.660
"EPIC night!!

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:23.848
Thanx for coming to see #Emancipation.

00:01:23.848 --> 00:01:24.910
Hope ya'll enjoyed!!"

00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:31.000
But what everyone wants to know is when the
public can actually see the historical film.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.470
According to Apple, "Emancipation" will get
a limited release in theaters on December

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:41.250
2 before hitting Apple TV+ on December 9,
which is right in time for awards season.

00:01:41.674 --> 00:01:47.830
Film experts and critics expect Will Smith,
despite his 2022 Oscar controversy, to still

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:50.920
be in the running for another gold statue
next year.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:55.630
Associate professor of film studies at the
University of Sydney, Bruce Isaacs, tweeted,

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:59.649
"There is HUGE oscar buzz around #Emancipation."

00:01:59.649 --> 00:02:04.509
For those who somehow missed what happened
at the Academy Awards, Smith basically turned

00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:07.610
his entire life and career upside down.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:12.330
He did so with a swift slap to the face of
comedian Chris Rock following a joke that

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.739
Rock made about Smith's wife, Jada Pinkett
Smith.

00:02:15.739 --> 00:02:20.610
Rock, who was on stage presenting an award,
had poked fun at Jada for having a shaved

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:26.090
head, which is reportedly due to alopecia.
Smith would later apologize for his actions

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:32.019
in an Instagram statement, saying his behavior
was, quote, "unacceptable and inexcusable."

00:02:32.019 --> 00:02:38.310
He also released a recorded response on YouTube
in July 2022 that featured multiple apologies

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:40.284
to both Rock and his family.

00:02:40.284 --> 00:02:43.523
"This is probably irreparable."

00:02:43.856 --> 00:02:48.800
With "Emancipation" not coming out until December,
many experts feel like there's been enough

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:54.260
time and apologies made by Smith for him to
snag another Best Actor award at next year's

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:57.150
Oscars, especially given the movie's subject
matter.

00:02:57.548 --> 00:03:03.430
"Emancipation" is a film that centers around
an enslaved man from the 1860s who escapes

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:08.790
from a plantation and travels across the swamps
of Louisiana to reunite with his family.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:13.340
It's ultimately based on the true story of
"Whipped Peter," who became famous during

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:18.709
that time period for a picture that was taken
of his severely scarred and whipped back.

00:03:18.709 --> 00:03:23.180
The image was published in the Independent
and Harper's Weekly and helped open the nation's

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:27.420
eyes to the horrors of slavery, especially
in the South.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:32.730
Screenwriter Bill Collage reportedly used
Civil War-era documents and Peter's very own

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:35.060
diaries to help pen the script.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:41.150
Back in September 2021, Will Smith spoke to
GQ magazine about what drew him to the "Emancipation"

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:45.709
story, despite being hesitant to do movies
about slavery in the past.

00:03:45.709 --> 00:03:49.810
This includes "Django Unchained," which he
famously turned down.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:50.970
Smith said,

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:54.860
"This was one that was about love and the
power of Black love.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:57.049
And that was something that I could rock with.

00:03:57.049 --> 00:04:01.720
We were going to make a story about how Black
love makes us invincible."

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:06.590
The official Apple Studios description of
"Emancipation" describes Smith's Peter as

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:12.640
a slave who manages to escape from a Louisiana
plantation after being almost whipped to death.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:13.670
It further reads,

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:19.680
"He had to outwit cold-blooded hunters and
the unforgiving swamps of Louisiana on a torturous

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:20.680
journey north."

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.330
In addition to Will Smith, "Emancipation"
is set to feature a number of familiar faces.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:31.910
"Hell or High Water" and "3:10 to Yuma" star
Ben Foster is slated to play a slave hunter

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:33.250
named Fassel.

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:38.509
Legendary character actor Steven Ogg will
be portraying a man named Sergeant Howard.

00:04:38.509 --> 00:04:44.750
Other cast members include Charmaine Bingwa,
Gilbert Owuor, Mustafa Shakir, Grant Harvey,

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:48.870
Timothy Hutton, Michael Luwoye, and several
others.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:54.919
Speaking to The Guardian in April 2022, Foster
described how the story that inspired "Emancipation"

00:04:54.919 --> 00:05:00.280
had really moved and resonated with him, especially
since he had just finished making Barry Levinson's

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:05.220
"The Survivor" about real-life Auschwitz survivor
and boxer Harry Haft.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:06.340
Foster said,

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:11.040
"[Emancipation's] about escaping the camps
that America created with the African American

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.130
slave trade.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:17.750
The stark similarities that I learned, particularly
after visiting Auschwitz, are staggering.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.310
We are students and, as students.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:24.940
It's not uncommon to feel a burden of new
information and it takes a moment to digest."

00:05:25.425 --> 00:05:30.940
Legendary "Training Day" director Antoine
Fuqua is ultimately the one responsible for

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:35.850
bringing "Emancipation" to life, with him
serving as both director and executive producer

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:36.850
on the film.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:41.259
He spoke to Deadline in-depth over the summer
about what inspired him to get in the director's

00:05:41.259 --> 00:05:46.229
chair and how the story of "Whipped Peter"
is something that moved him both as a filmmaker

00:05:46.229 --> 00:05:47.430
and a person.

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:48.539
Fuqua said,

00:05:48.539 --> 00:05:53.289
"It was the first viral image of the brutality
of slavery that the world saw.

00:05:53.289 --> 00:05:56.660
It's almost two years now from when I first
read the script.

00:05:56.660 --> 00:06:01.069
It hit my heart and my soul in so many ways
that are impossible to convey."

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:05.480
He explained how Whipped Peter's story hit
close to home because of its family themes

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:10.372
— Peter, after all, wouldn't give up until
he returned to his family.

00:06:10.372 --> 00:06:13.137
"I will look in triumph…

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:16.774
at my enemies."

